ext" "the origin"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334290" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "infinity"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334290" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334290" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "a<0"] ["math" "a>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334290" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "a>0"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=8x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/r