n" "q-334314" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a center"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-2<0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable spiral (sink)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85d6cc71a17ffa258f3fef4d69c46a490d6a247f7c9046f246e70752f10489cc"} {"question" "q-334307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0"] ["math" "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0"] ["math" "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0"] ["math" "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0"] ["math" "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0"] ["math" "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334307" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334307" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a center"] ["text" "a stable spiral (sink)"] ["text" "an unstable spiral (source)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334307" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a stable spiral (sink)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-7y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =4\\pm 7\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "85d6cc71a17ffa258f3fef4d69c46a490d6a247f7c9046f246e70752f10489cc"} {"question" "q-334304" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334304" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0"] ["math" "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0"] ["math" "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0"] ["math" "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0"] ["math" "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0"] ["math" "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334304" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334304" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334304" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a center"] ["text" "a stable spiral (sink)"] ["text" "an unstable spiral (source)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "q-334304" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an unstable spiral (source)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/state.json" "file_sha256" "baea455fb2924dc2cf1299d5f2336376c259449922c26af6dc859032416a3c87"} {"question" "e-21657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/6feea8db62000e44334251a96c39bd8d0d8d7b1ec9a61380f3a5b4b613ea6c80.png)\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/example-21657.json" "file_sha256" "4b7c7262421ecc8b697bbc70c7b24086df869e1eef0f58e54d33b896110cd67b"} {"question" "e-21657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We are given that the general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/bceb494a6baeca8695157b4fd285b2880a242367747c0d72a0f573612ef87652.png)\n\nFor example, when $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2.12$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/3c2ea4467854f749e07f5ae3922273a2ebd441da4fb9df834ce0676157e856a2.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(3t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(3t)| \\leq 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is bounded. So, we have an ellipse that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/df8e7ae5f7ff7281cbdbf5849dc94faf59d2421621dc5009b21eeb947485d153.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/example-21657.json" "file_sha256" "4b7c7262421ecc8b697bbc70c7b24086df869e1eef0f58e54d33b896110cd67b"} {"question" "e-21658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda = a \\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Provide an interpretation of the portrait and determine the possible values of $a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/6d13d0dfadac45d40b74cb7e740a880305098f0939e2f45d74706c4022183c54.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/example-21658.json" "file_sha256" "70d837b83c560b016d3dfd79f2964f75b4f03a80cef5075cc2d42739a4d39b44"} {"question" "e-21658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is unbounded, representing a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise. Thus, we must have $a \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward the origin.\n\nHence, to insure that $e^{at} \\to 0,$ we require $a < 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/example-21658.json" "file_sha256" "70d837b83c560b016d3dfd79f2964f75b4f03a80cef5075cc2d42739a4d39b44"} {"question" "e-21659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda=\\pm2\\text{i}.$ Provide an interpretation of the equilibrium point (origin) for the system of differential equations given above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/example-21659.json" "file_sha256" "178d770680a619bc34c83a39116c3f2331ab64778a70c237a1a245cf110b8acf"} {"question" "e-21659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0$ and $e^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is a center." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/example-21659.json" "file_sha256" "178d770680a619bc34c83a39116c3f2331ab64778a70c237a1a245cf110b8acf"}] "usage" {"e-21658" [] "q-334290" [] "e-21659" [] "e-21657" [] "q-334307" []} "basis" 2398 "retractions" [[17592186167180 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3241/Source/Images/q-334290.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $λ = a ± b\\textrm{i}$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The given curve is\n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed\n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have\n{{field-4}}."] [17592186167181 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-2x(t) - 5y(t); y^{′}(t) = 5x(t) - 2y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ =-2 ± 5\\textrm{i}$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\nSince\n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186167181 :question/answer-fields 17592186164499] [17592186166482 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n${x^{′}(t) =-y(t); y^{′}(t) = 9x(t)$\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i}$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T$, respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above."] [17592186166482 :question/worked-solution "We are given that the general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFor example, when $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0$, we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{x =-\\sin (3t); y = 3\\cos (3t)\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2.12$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3241/Source/Images/53de9c32f79277beb9f683133f7b0cf5.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\mid \\sin(3t) \\mid \\leq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mid \\cos(3t) \\mid \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThus, the obtained curve is bounded. So, we have an ellipse that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3241/Source/Images/3d7278b105e7e6f65eb433ca671c6a4e.png)"] [17592186166483 :question/problem "The phase portrait for