f{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}\n$$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{complex}(t) &= ve^{(a + b\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\n&= e^{at}(\\cos (bt) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (bt))(p + q\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= e^{at}(\\cos (bt)p - \\sin (bt)q) + \\textrm{i}e^{at}(\\sin (bt)p + \\cos (bt)q)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = c_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + c_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\na = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ne^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1\n$$\n\nfor all $t$. Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin.\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0\n$$\n\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is a center."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186164487 17592186164488 17592186164490 17592186164491 17592186164493 17592186164494 17592186164496 17592186164497 17592186164498 17592186164500 17592186164501 17592186164502 17592186164503 17592186164504 17592186164505 17592186164507 17592186164508 17592186164509] "immutable_fields" [[17592186164486 :answer-field/id] [17592186164486 :answer-field/key] [17592186164486 :answer-field/type] [17592186164489 :answer-field/id] [17592186164489 :answer-field/key] [17592186164489 :answer-field/type] [17592186164492 :answer-field/id] [17592186164492 :answer-field/key] [17592186164492 :answer-field/type] [17592186164495 :answer-field/id] [17592186164495 :answer-field/key] [17592186164495 :answer-field/type] [17592186164499 :answer-field/id] [175921861